From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winex11, win32u: a maximized Linux program dragged by its own title bar is restored under the pointer

A Linux program that draws its own title bar (SG Office, Firefox with its
tabs in the title bar, a GTK program's header bar), maximized in its Wine
frame, could not be dragged out of being maximized: pressed and dragged,
its title bar did nothing. The program asks the window manager to move it
(_NET_WM_MOVERESIZE); the frame grabs the pointer and starts Wine's move
(0803, 0818), but Wine drags a maximized window out of being maximized only
with the left button down as Wine knows it -- and Wine never saw it go
down, the press having gone on to the program.

With the pointer grabbed for the frame and the left button still down, the
frame's thread is now told the button is down before its move starts. Then
the move is Wine's own for a maximized window (0750): nothing until the
pointer moves past the drag distance (a click, or the program's own
double-click, which maximizes and restores it itself, is left alone); then
the frame is restored, under the pointer -- as far across its title bar,
in proportion, as the pointer was across the maximized one, and as far
below its top -- and follows the pointer until the button comes up. The
button's release comes to the frame (its grab), so the move always ends,
and the grab with it (0818).

And Wine's own drag out of being maximized let go of the pointer while it
restored the window: the button let go just then went to the window under
the pointer -- for a framed program, the program -- and the move never
ended, the window following the pointer, every click its, until another
click. The pointer now stays held until the move takes it on.

Test: test/embeddragmax-gate.sh, with test/ownframe-client.c (now selecting
the button's release, as Qt and GTK do; OWNFRAME_DBLCLICK makes its title
bar's double-click toggle maximized, as theirs does) and
test/embeddragmax-slowrestore.c (a CBT hook holding the restore 1.5 s).
Mutants: SG_MUTANT_EMBED_NO_DRAG_UNMAXIMIZE (dlls/winex11.drv/sg_embed.c)
fails the drag; SG_MUTANT_DRAG_UNMAXIMIZE_UNHELD (dlls/win32u/defwnd.c)
fails the release during the restore.
---
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/sg_embed.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/sg_embed.c
@@ -1141,6 +1141,29 @@
                         usleep( 10000 );
                     }
                     TRACE( "move/size %ld: pointer grab %d\n", dir, st );
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_EMBED_NO_DRAG_UNMAXIMIZE
+                    /* Maximized, its own title bar dragged: restored under the
+                     * pointer and moved, as a maximized window dragged by its
+                     * title bar is (win32u's drag_unmaximize) -- which goes
+                     * only with the left button down as Wine knows it, and
+                     * Wine never saw it go down (the press was replayed to
+                     * the program): the frame's thread is told it is. With
+                     * the pointer ours now, its release comes to the frame
+                     * (and ends the drag, or the wait for one: a click alone,
+                     * or the program's double-click, leaves it maximized). */
+                    if (dir == 8 && st == GrabSuccess && (NtUserGetWindowLongW( hwnd, GWL_STYLE ) & WS_MAXIMIZE))
+                    {
+                        BYTE keys[256];
+                        XQueryPointer( display, DefaultRootWindow( display ), &root_ret, &child_ret,
+                                       &rx, &ry, &wx, &wy, &mask );   /* not let go before the grab */
+                        if ((mask & Button1Mask) && NtUserGetKeyboardState( keys ))
+                        {
+                            keys[VK_LBUTTON] |= 0x80;
+                            NtUserSetKeyboardState( keys );
+                            TRACE( "maximized: dragged out of it\n" );
+                        }
+                    }
+#endif
                     if (dir == 8) NtUserPostMessage( hwnd, WM_SYSCOMMAND, SC_MOVE | HTCAPTION, 0 );
                     else NtUserPostMessage( hwnd, WM_SYSCOMMAND, SC_SIZE | size_edge[dir], 0 );
 #ifndef SG_MUTANT_EMBED_MOVESIZE_GRAB_LEAK
--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -707,11 +707,22 @@
         }
     }
     pt = msg.pt;
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_DRAG_UNMAXIMIZE_UNHELD
     set_capture_window( 0, GUI_INMOVESIZE, NULL );
+#endif
 
+    /* Stained Glass OS: the capture (the pointer grabbed) is kept while it is
+     * restored, until the move takes it on: let go meanwhile, the button's
+     * release could go to another window -- for a Linux program's window in
+     * its frame (winex11 sg_embed.c), to the program -- and the move never
+     * ended, the window following the pointer until a click */
     get_window_rect( hwnd, &max_rect, get_thread_dpi() );
     NtUserShowWindow( hwnd, SW_RESTORE );
-    if (is_zoomed( hwnd )) return FALSE;
+    if (is_zoomed( hwnd ))
+    {
+        set_capture_window( 0, GUI_INMOVESIZE, NULL );
+        return FALSE;
+    }
     get_window_rect( hwnd, &rect, get_thread_dpi() );
     w = rect.right - rect.left;
     h = rect.bottom - rect.top;
